Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Neuropsychological impairment (NPI) has been shown to occur at epidemic levels in the current stage of the combination antiretroviral therapy (cART) era. The stages of HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder (HAND) recapitulate the systemic stages of HIV disease progression (asymptomatic, early symptomatic, non-AIDS-defining, and late symptomatic/AIDS). Neuroprotection should be considered in the prescription of initial ARV regimens. HAND remains a set of diagnoses requiring exclusion of both HIV-associated and non-HIV-associated neurologic disease; a complete workup using CT scan or MRI of the head; lumbar puncture; and exclusion of metabolic causes, prescribed medication toxicities, psychoactive substance use diagnoses, major depressive disorder, and other potentially confounding psychiatric disorders. Lack of ARV adherence that is caused by HAND is a special concern, as it may culminate in a "reverberating circuit" of worsening cognitive function over time. Although formal clinical trial evidence for the treatment of HAND remains limited in the current cART era, prioritization may be assigned for use of the CNS-penetrating ARVs and the psychostimulants. Supportive evidence exists for the use of CNS anti-inflammatory agents aimed at both brain tissue and the vasculature, selected neurotransmitter manipulation, anti-neurodegenerative agents, and cognitive rehabilitative techniques. The development of treatments aimed at reducing the CNS reservoir of latent virus and at viral eradication from the CNS are targets for the future. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
