Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Comments on an article by Jennifer S. Albrecht et al. (see record 2013-25748-002). Albrecht et al. compares quality of hospice care for individuals with and without dementia in a national sample. Quality was measured in this study by identifying relevant metrics collected in the 2007 National Home and Hospice Care Survey. The authors, following the National Consensus Project for Quality Palliative Care guidelines chose report of depression, report of pain at the last assessment in hospice care, presence of standing orders for pain medication if pain was reported, greater than Stage 2 pressure ulcers, use of antibiotics, tube feeding, lack of continuity of residence, emergency care, hospice for 3 days or less, presence of a do-not-resuscitate order, and presence of any advanced directive as measures. The current study says nothing about quality of care and quality of life for the two-thirds of persons dying with dementia who do not receive hospice care. Reasons for low use of hospice of individuals with dementia are unknown but probably include prognostic uncertainty, the requirement for residential care and nursing home facility staff to call in an outside team to care for their residents, and the failure to recognize dementia as the terminal illness that it is. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
