A2  - Boudreaux, E. D., Cagande, C., Kilgannon, H., et al., Boudreaux, Conwell, Y., Van Orden, K., Caine, E. D., Conwell, Erlangsen, A., Nordentoft, M., Conwell, Y., et al., Erlangsen, Gonzalez, O., Berry, J. T., McKnight-Eily, L. R., et al., Gonzalez, Lapierre, S., Erlangsen, A., Waern, M., et al., Lapierre, Larkin, G. L., Claassen, C. A., Emond, J. A., et al., Larkin, Meldon, S. W., Emerman, C. L., Schubert, D. S., Meldon, Morrison, K. B., Laing, L., Morrison, Screening for suicide risk: A systematic evidence review for the US Preventive Services Task Force, Szanto, K., Gildengers, A., Mulsant, B. H., et al., Szanto
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Presents a case report of unexpected suicidality in an older individual in an emergency department. A 66-year-old white man presented to an emergency department (ED) with a foot wound that he had sustained the previous day from stepping on a nail while gardening. The patient received a diphtheria, tetanus, and pertussis booster and was started on a course of oral antibiotics. As part of a newly instituted ED protocol, his nurse asked him standard screening questions concerning recent depression and SI. The ED mental health team evaluated him and admitted him to a psychiatric hospital. Upon discharge, he was on a stable dose of sertraline, had reestablished contact with his primary care provider, and had no further SI. Most EDs rely on targeted SI questioning triggered by clinician concern, but some have begun standard screening of all patients, in part because of Joint Commission patient safety goals related to suicide prevention. Suicide prevention for older adults poses unique challenges because older suicidal individuals are more likely to employ advance planning and less likely to directly ask for help, with a suicide attempt to completion ratio in older adults of approximately 4:1, compared with up to 200:1 in young adults. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
