Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Presents a case report of a 65 year old Caucasian female professor with a history notable for hypertension and depression presented with a change in mental status in the context of severe hypertension. This case highlights the importance of a broad differential diagnosis when approaching new-onset manic symptoms among geriatric patients in an inpatient setting. She had experienced several days of euphoria, insomnia, excessive journaling, pressured speech, inappropriate laughter, and grandiose ideas about inventing a new school of philosophy. When she arrived at the hospital, she was acutely confused, disoriented, and agitated. She had waxing and waning levels of alertness and was non-verbal. She was afebrile with a blood pressure (BP) of 212/119 mmHg. Physical exam was notable for a nonfocal neurologic exam with normal muscle tone and fundi. Her BP was aggressively managed, and by the second hospital day, her confusion and disorientation cleared, though she continued to exhibit grandiosity, mood lability, and loose associations. Extensive medical workup was unremarkable, including magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), electroencephalography (EEG), cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis, porphyrin tests, infectious screens except for a urinary tract infection (UTI), which was treated with antibiotics. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
