A2  - Dunn, N.R., Alfonso, C.A., Young, R.A., Isakov, G., Lefer, J., Dunn, Geschwind, M.D., Geschwind, Jardri, R., DiPaola, C., LaJugie, C., Thomas, P., Goeb, J.L., Jardri, Kovacs, G.G., Puopolo, M., Ladogana, A., Pocchiari, M., Budka, H., van Duijn, C., Kovacs, Ladogana, A., Puopolo, M., Croes, E.A., Budka, H., Jarius, C., Collins, S., et al., Ladogana, Parchi, P., Giese, A., Capellari, S., Brown, P., Schulz-Schaeffer, W., Windl, O., et al., Parchi, Rabinovici, G.D., Wang, P.N., Levin, J., Cook, L., Pravdin, M., Davis, J., et al., Rabinovici, Reeves, R.R., Torres, R.A., Reeves, Roos, R., Gajdusek, D.C., Gibbs, C.J., Jr, Roos, Shiga, Y., Miyazawa, K., Fukushima, R., Shibuya, S., Sato, Y., Konno, H., et al., Shiga, Steinhoff, B.J., Zerr, I., Glatting, M., Schulz-Schaeffer, W., Poser, S., Kretzschmar, H.A., Steinhoff, Wall, C.A., Rummans, T.A., Aksamit, A.J., Krahn, L.E., Pankratz, V.S., Wall, Wieser, H.G., Schwarz, U., Blattler, T., Bernoulli, C., Sitzler, M., Stoeck, K., et al., Wieser, World Health Organization, Young, G.S., Geschwind, M.D., Fishbein, N.J., Martindale, J.L., Henry, R.G., Liu, S., et al., Young, Zerr, I., Pocchiari, M., Collins, S., Brandel, J.P., de Pedro Cuesta, J., Knight, R.S., et al., Zerr
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Presents a case report of a 61-year-old Hispanic man with a 22 year history of schizophrenia was brought in by his family to our institution's emergency department (ED) after a fall at home. He had been previously independent in all activities of daily living until a few weeks prior to presentation, when he developed gait unsteadiness resulting in falls, anxiety, worsening depression, and globally blurred vision, all of which were slowly progressive. Four days prior to admission, he developed severe insomnia. The family reported that Mr. A had a long history of schizophrenia with at least three psychiatric admissions. He had no known medical conditions. At the time of his presentation, he was taking haloperidol 10 mg nightly, bupropion SR 200 mg twice daily, duloxetine 90 mg nightly, ziprasidone 80 mg daily and 160 mg nightly, and benztropine (unclear dosing), which was an unchanged regimen for at least 2 years. His compliance was questioned as his family did not supervise his medication administration. In the ED he was started on broad-spectrum antibiotics for a suspected infection and given a total of 6 mg of oral lorazepam for possible alcohol withdrawal, given his history of alcohol abuse and autonomic lability. On his fourth day in the psychiatry inpatient unit, he began to have multifocal myoclonus of his extremities. Though no other change in mental status and neurological exam occurred, the new finding of myoclonus raised suspicion for an alternative diagnosis, and he was transferred to the inpatient neurology service. Ophthalmologic consultation failed to reveal any ocular abnormalities to explain his apparent left hemianopsia. A repeat MRI 8 days later showed hyperintensities on diffusion weighted imaging in the occipital lobes bilaterally, which on closer examination were also present on the original MRI. While on the neurology service, he became increasingly more agitated with physically aggressive behavior. Lorazepam as needed (averaging less than 2 mg per day) and ziprasidone (from 20 to 60 mg daily) were added to haloperidol with limited success. Approximately 1 month after presentation, Mr. A stopped answering questions, following commands, and was akinetic. Six weeks after his presentation to the ED, he was transferred to a chronic care facility. To our knowledge, this is the first report of sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (sCJD) in a patient with long-standing schizophrenia. This case illustrates how pre-existing schizophrenia can complicate the diagnosis of sCJD. As sCJD can present with psychiatric symptoms, sCJD should be considered even in well documented schizophrenia when there is rapid deterioration in cognition with fleeting psychiatric symptoms and the presence of neurological symptoms. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
