A2  - Antinori, A., Ammassari, A., De Luca, A., et al., Antinori, Artico, M., Pastore, F. S., Polosa, M., Sherkat, S., Neroni, M., Artico, Burke, D. S., Brundage, J. F., Redfield, R. R., et al., Burke, Buhring, U., Herrlinger, U., Krings, T., Thiex, R., Weller, M., Kuker, W., Buhring, Del Brutto, O. H., Rajshekhar, V., White, A. C., Jr., et al., Del Brutto, Dunn, W., Jamil, L. H., Brown, L. S., et al., Dunn, Idem, Ishak, K., Baptista, A., Bianchi, L., et al., Ishak, Katlama, C., De Wit, S., O'Doherty, E., Van Glabeke, M., Clumeck, N., Katlama, Luft, B. J., Hafner, R., Korzun, A. H., et al., Luft, Mallal, S., Phillips, E., Carosi, G., et al., Mallal, Omuro, A. M., Leite, C. C., Mokhtari, K., Delattre, J. Y., Omuro, Raffi, F., Aboulker, J. P., Michelet, C., et al., Raffi, Sax, P. E., Islam, R., Walensky, R. P., et al., Sax, Schackman, B. R., Scott, C. A., Walensky, R. P., Losina, E., Freedberg, K. A., Sax, P. E., Schackman, Scheld, W. M., Whitley, R. J., Durack, D. T., Scheld, Slone, H. W., Blake, J. J., Shah, R., Guttikonda, S., Bourekas, E. C., Slone, Zolopa, A., Andersen, J., Powderly, W., et al., Zolopa
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - [Correction Notice: An erratum for this article was reported in Vol 366(14) of The New England Journal of Medicine (see record 2012-09076-005). In the original article, in Syndromic Diagnosis subsection of Differential Diagnosis (page 748), the final sentence of the first paragraph should have read, "However, anisocoria with the right pupil larger than the left pupil suggests dysfunction of the right pupillary constrictor muscles, the parasympathetic component of the right oculomotor nerve, or the left sympathetic pathway," rather than ". . . suggests dysfunction of the right optic nerve, the right pupillary constrictor muscles . . . ." The article is corrected at NEJM.org.] Presents the case report of a 45-year-old man with a history of alcohol abuse, rapid cognitive decline and worsening jaundice. During the previous 3 months, increasing fatigue and cough productive of yellow sputum and flecks of blood had developed, with post-tussive vomiting. On examination, the patient was lethargic but oriented to person, place, and time and able to follow simple commands. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) after the administration of contrast material revealed a solitary lesion in the right insula, with thin, smooth, peripheral enhancement and extensive surrounding edema and mild mass effect. He had a history of elevated results of liver-function tests, anxiety, depression, gout, erectile dysfunction, and bursitis of the right shoulder; he had undergone a hemorrhoidectomy 7 years earlier after an episode of rectal bleeding and iron-deficiency anemia requiring hospitalization. He had a long history of alcohol abuse (up to 1 liter of vodka per day). He had declined detoxification treatment, disulfiram therapy, and referral to support groups for alcoholism. He did not smoke or use illicit drugs. Medications included allopurinol, citalopram, sildenafil, and vitamins; he had taken celecoxib for bursitis for 3 months, stopping 3 months earlier. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
