Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Presents a case report of a 52-year-old African American who was human immunodeficiency virus-positive and had a history of cocaine abuse, post-traumatic stress disorder, and depression. Sarah was admitted to the hospital for severe otitis media and mastoiditis. One day later, she was discharged on antibiotics and tramadol for pain; Sarah reported that the new pain medicine helped her sleep better. Sarah was readmitted a second time within a few days in respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation. Her mental status was altered and she became combative. Sarah developed neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) following haloperidol administration and her temperature climbed to 106.9degreeF. Four vasopressor agents were required, and despite supportive care and aggressive resuscitation, her lactic acid climbed and she did not perfuse. Sarah died shortly thereafter. Cause of death was considered to be NMS. This case illuminates the need to better understand the pathophysiology of NMS and serotonin syndrome in light of the common use of psychotropics in the United States. This is particularly true if other risk factors for NMS are present. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
