Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome (ME/CFS) affects as many as 1 in 100 women in the United States, but its pathogenesis remains largely unknown. ME/CFS is associated with depression, inability to concentrate, and pain, necessitating the concurrent use of antidepressants, especially serotonin-selective reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). Considerable evidence, however, suggests that the older tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) may be more efficacious than SSRIs in ME/CFS9Y and in fibromyalgia. Moreover, TCAs are also effective in managing chronic pain. Evidence presented in this issue further indicates that TCAs can also inhibit activation of mast cells. These unique immune cells that are found in all tissues, but also especially in the diencephalon, were hypothesized to be involved in ME/CFS. It has been well known that SSRIs, but not the TCAs, increase the risk of gastrointestinal bleeding when given alone and further increase this risk when administered together with NSAIDs. However, the underlying mechanism is still unknown. Interestingly, TCAs do not seem to do so maybe because of their antagonism of histamine-1 receptors that are known to be protective against gastritis. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
