A2  - Horacek, J., Brunovsky, M., Novak, T., Tislerova, B., Palenicek, T., Bubenikova-Valesova, V., Spaniel, F., Koprivova, J., Mohr, P., Balikova, M., Hoschl, C., Horacek, Myint, A. M., Schwarz, M. J., Steinbusch, H. W., Leonard, B. E., Myint, Najmanova, V., Rambousek, L., Syslova, K., Bubenikova, V., Slamberova, R., Vales, K., Kacer, P., Najmanova, Yang, J. J., Zhou, Z. Q., Yang, C., Yang
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Reply by the current authors to the comments made by Jian Jun Yang (see record 2011-17192-021) on the original article (see record 2010-18383-004). The commentators have offered an alternative explanation for the fast antidepressant effect of ketamine. Their interpretation suggests an inhibitory effect of ketamine on pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1 and TNF-alpha) which are upregulated in depression. Owing the fact that IL-1 and TNF-alpha also enhance the serotonin (5-HT) transporter, the anti inflammatory effect of ketamine would be involved in its antidepressant activity. To evaluate this hypothesis, we studied the influence of ketamine in male Wistar/ Hann rats on both proinflammatory cytokines (IL-1 and TNF-a) and 5-HT activity in the brain. Interestingly, we found that the hippocampal 5-HT levels correlate negatively with the basal TNF-alpha and positively with the absolute and relative change of this cytokine in the whole sample. Similar significant findings were also confirmed for the ketamine group. This observation supports Yang's assumption that TNF-alpha suppresses 5-HT level, and its inhibition by ketamine facilitates 5-HT. However, we did not detect any correlation between 5-HTIAA levels and TNF-alpha and therefore our data do not correspond with the inhibition of 5-HT transporter mediating the influence of TNF-alpha on 5-HT in the brain. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
