N2  - Presents a case report of a 75-year-old man with no past psychiatric history had been diagnosed with colorectal cancer and was treated with surgical resection and chemotherapy, which was thought to be curative. His postoperative course was complicated by high ostomy output and a wound infection that developed 10 days after his operation. He was readmitted to the hospital for wound drainage and management. Cultures grew E. coli and Enterococci, and he was started on ampicillin and metronidazole, which were subsequently switched to levofloxacin and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX). Twelve hours following his third dose of levofloxacin and fifth dose of TMP-SMX, he developed acute onset of nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and severe depression. On admission, the levofloxacin and TMP-SMX were discontinued and intravenous fluids were administered. Within 48 h of stopping the antibiotics, his symptoms started to improve. Fluoroquinolones are important medications in the armamentarium against multiple infections. Despite their general safety, it is necessary to maintain awareness of rare and serious side-effects. We believe that the acute onset of severe depression and suicidality in our patient was related to levofloxacin. Our hope is that reporting this case will lead to future early recognition and prompt resolution of distressing medication-induced side-effects. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2024 APA, all rights reserved)
