A2  - Alexopoulos, G. S., Meyers, B. S., Young, R. C., et al., Alexopoulos, Beekman, A. T., Copeland, J. R., Prince, M. J., Beekman, Belmaker, R. H., Agam, G., Belmaker, Craddock, D., Thomas, A., Craddock, Devanand, D. P., Adorno, E., Cheng, J., et al., Devanand, Eikelenboom, P., Hoogendijk, W. J., Jonker, C., et al., Eikelenboom, Irwin, M. R., Miller, A. H., Irwin, Jongenelis, K., Pot, A. M., Eisses, A. M., et al., Jongenelis, Mody, L., Miller, D. K., McGloin, J. M., et al., Mody, Raison, C. L., Capuron, L., Miller, A. H., Raison, Smith, R. S., Smith, Tyring, S., Gottlieb, A., Papp, K., et al., Tyring
Y1  - 2010//
N2  - In the treatment of autoimmune diseases such as psoriasis, Crohn disease, and rheumatoid arthritis, tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-alpha) antagonists are being used to reduce inflammation. Therefore, we aimed to investigate the effectiveness of TNF-alpha antagonist infliximab for late-onset depressive disorders, in the absence of a prominent autoimmune disease. We set up a randomized placebo-controlled trial in which patients 60 years and older with a late-onset antidepressant-resistant depressive disorder were randomized to either a single 3-mg/kg dose of infliximab intravenously or a placebo infusion. It is possible that the single, rather low dose of 3-mg/kg infliximab that was given in our study was suboptimal, thus failing to demonstrate positive results. In comparison, autoimmune diseases such as psoriasis, Crohn disease, and rheumatoid arthritis are being treated with repeated infusions of infliximab in a dose of 5 mg/kg. Further research is needed to determine the possible effectiveness of immune antagonists on depressive disorders and comorbid depression in autoimmune diseases. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2024 APA, all rights reserved)
