., Vaccarino, den Biggelaar, van, A. H., Gussekloo, J., de Craen, A. J., Frolich, M., Stek, M. L., der Mast, van, R. C., Westendorp, R. G., den Biggelaar, der Most, van, P. J., Dolga, A. M., Nijholt, I. M., Luiten, P. G., Eisel, U. L., der Most, Wardle, J., Armitage, J., Collins, R., Wallendszus, K., Keech, A., Lawson, A., Wardle, Yang, C. C., Jick, S. S., Jick, H., Yang, Young-Xu, Y., Chan, K. A., Liao, J. K., Ravid, S., Blatt, C. M., Young-Xu
Y1  - 2010//
N2  - The notion that inflammation and inflammatory mediators increase major depressive disorder (MDD) vulnerability is well established. As statins and aspirin both have anti-inflammatory properties, we hypothesized that therapy with these agents would reduce the risk for depression. From an age-stratified sample of 1,494 women, 837 were aged at least 50 years, meeting the inclusion criterion for this study. The primary design was a nested case-control study. Cases were subjects with first-episode MDD, controls were MDD free. Use of statins and aspirin was recognized if exposure preceded MDD onset for cases and the 10-year assessment for controls. A retrospective cohort analysis was also performed for subjects with no prior history of MDD who were followed from baseline or time of exposure to statins or aspirin, until a first episode of MDD or the 10-year follow up. Results indicated that the prevalence of exposure to statins and aspirin was lower among women with MDD. Exposure to non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents, oral glucocorticoids, paracetamol, hormone therapy and antidepressants did not explain the protective effect of statins and aspirin on the development of MDD. Thus, we report that exposure to statins and aspirin appears to be associated with a reduced risk for MDD. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
