T1  - Induction of manic switch by the tumour necrosis factor-alpha antagonist infliximab.
A1  - Brietzke, Elisa
A1  - Lafer, Beny
AI  - Lafer, Beny; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6132-9999
A2  - Brietzke, E., Kapczinski, F., Brietzke, Brietzke, E., Stertz, L., Fernandes, B., et al., Brietzke, Jackson, J. M., Jackson, Kaufman, K. R., Kaufman, O'Brien, S. M., Scully, P., Scott, L. V., Dinan, T. G., O'Brien, Soczynska, J. K., Kennedy, S. H., Goldstein, B. I., Lachowski, A., Woldeyohannes, H. O., McIntyre, R. S., Soczynska
Y1  - 2010//
N2  - Presents a case report of a 43-year-old woman with mild and long-lasting depressive symptoms that started in adolescence and a clinical picture compatible with dysthymia. The patient had never sought psychiatric assistance for these symptoms. At the age of 41, she had a major depressive episode and began to take citalopram. Depressive symptoms remitted and the medication regimen was maintained. Later she presented with severe gastrointestinal complaints, and treatment with infliximab was prescribed. Two days after the first infliximab infusion, she became progressively talkative and agitated. In four days, she had a euphoric mood, increased energy, racing thoughts and was markedly talkative and socially inconvenient. Infliximab was changed to mesalazine and manic and hypomanic episodes never developed again.. This case implicates that TNF-alpha antagonist is involved in the development of manic switches. Although the role of TNF-alpha in the pathophysiology of depressive and manic episodes is still unclear, TNF-alpha antagonists, as well as other immunomodulatory medications, have been studied as reasonable alternatives in the treatment of depressive symptoms in recent years. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
