A2  - Chen, F., Larsen, M. B., Sanchez, C., Wiborg, O., Chen, Duijvestijn, Y. C., Kalmeijer, M. D., Passier, A. L., Dahlem, P., Smiers, F., Duijvestijn, Kennedy, S. H., Andersen, H. F., Lam, R. W., Kennedy, Kharofa, J., Sekar, P., Haverbusch, M., Moomaw, C., Flaherty, M., Kissela, B., et al., Kharofa, Lake, M. B., Birmaher, B., Wassick, S., Mathos, K., Yelovich, A. K., Lake, Loke, Y. K., Trivedi, A. N., Singh, S., Loke, Martin, K. A., Krahn, L. E., Balan, V., Rosati, M. J., Martin, Movig, K. L., Janssen, M. W., De Waal Malefijt, J., Kabel, P. J., Leufkens, H. G., Egberts, A. C., Movig, Mowla, A., Dastgheib, S. A., Ebrahimi, A. A., Pani, A., Mowla, Naranjo, C. A., Busto, U., Sellers, E. M., Sandor, P., Ruiz, I., Roberts, E. A., et al., Naranjo, Schalekamp, T., Klungel, O. H., Souverein, P. C., de Boer, A., Schalekamp, Serebruany, V. L., Serebruany, Sharma, R. C., Sharma, Shen, W. W., Swartz, C. M., Calhoun, J. W., Shen, Sugiyama, N., Sasayama, D., Amano, N., Sugiyama, Van Walraven, C., Mamdani, M. M., Wells, P. S., Williams, J. I., Van Walraven
Y1  - 2010//
N2  - Escitalopram, one of the newest Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitor (SSRI) available, has been reported to be slightly more effective than other SSRIs in adults with major depressive disorder. SSRI's are the most common drugs used for anxiety and depressive disorders, both by psychiatrists and physicians. Although SSRIs exhibit a favorable safety profile, some case reports and studies associate SSRIs with increased risk of bleeding events. Caution has been advised about the risk of bleeding associated with co-prescription of escitalopram with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), aspirin, or other medications that affect coagulation, in patients with liver disease and those undergoing surgery. Most of the case reports have been related to gastrointestinal (GI) bleed. SSRI-related abnormal bleeding is infrequently reported from non-GI sites. We report a case of escitalopram related epistaxis in a 31 years old male with depressive disorder. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
