Y1  - 2009//
N2  - Cyclic vomiting syndrome (CVS) is a potentially incapacitating disorder that affects all ages. First recognized in children, it has been increasingly documented in adults as well. CVS is characterized by episodes of high-intensity nausea and vomiting lasting for several days, recurring several times per year, with symptom-free intervals in between episodes. The prevalence in adults is currently unknown, and its pathophysiology remains speculative. In adults, CVS may be a primary disorder, secondary to systemic disease, or a consequence of cannabinoid abuse. Therapy is symptomatic and includes tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs), triptans, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, benzodiazepines, and antiemetics. Some patients with CVS have increased psychiatric comorbidity with anxiety or depression. A prior column provided an overview and history of CVS, its epidemiology, the clinical features of CVS in children and adults, and the Rome III diagnostic criteria for CVS. The current column describes psychiatric aspects of the illness, its pathophysiology, differential diagnosis and diagnostic evaluation, a particular variant of CVS (cannabinoid hyperemesis), treatment, and prognosis. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
