A2  - Chen, W., Mahadomrongkul, V., Berger, U. V., Bassan, M., DeSilva, T., Tanaka, K., et al., Chen, Contractor, A., Swanson, G., Heinemann, S. F., Contractor, Omrani, A., Melone, M., Bellesi, M., Safiulina, V., Aida, T., Tanaka, K., Cherubini, E., Conti, F., Omrani, Rothstein, J. D., Patel, S., Regan, M. R., Haenggeli, C., Huang, Y. H., Bergles, D. E., et al., Rothstein, Yokoi, M., Kobayashi, K., Manabe, T., Takahashi, T., Sakaguchi, I., Katsuura, G., et al., Yokoi
Y1  - 2009//
N2  - Comments on an article by Azar Omrani et al. (see record 2011-11933-004). Omrani and colleagues present evidence that regulating the amount of glutamate uptake can have a dramatic impact on the ability of mossy fibre synapses to express long-term depression (LTD). The authors make use of an interesting observation that some beta-lactam antibiotics can transcriptionally up-regulate the expression of the glutamate transporter GLT-1, a process that might hold some promise for reducing excitotoxicity in certain neuropathologies. The authors demonstrate that the expression of GLT-1 is increased in the brains of rats which are treated with the beta-lactam antibiotic ceftriaxone. GLT-1 is generally thought to be an astroglial transporter; however, more recently it has been demonstrated that it may also be expressed in neurons, and more importantly, in excitatory synaptic terminals in the hippocampus. In agreement with this, Omrani et al. found that immunogold particles labeled both astrocyte processes and mossy fibre terminals in the stratum lucidum. In animals that were treated with ceftriaxone the density of both cytoplasmic and membrane-associated particles was increased. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
