T1  - Response to "rTMS in headache prophylaxis: When case reports hide our headache."
A1  - O'Reardon, John P
A1  - Fontecha, Jeisson F
A1  - Cristancho, Mario A

A2  - O'Reardon, J. P., Fontecha, J. F., Cristancho, M. A., Newman, S., O'Reardon
Y1  - 2008//
N2  - Reply by the current author to the comments made by A. Fumal, A. Maertens, and J. Schoenen (see record 2008-15913-002) on the original article (see record 2008-15888-006). We thank Fumal and colleagues for their interest in our paper and the pertinent questions they have raised. The washout of psychotropics prior to enrollment in the repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS) trial in major depression did not include non-sterodial anti-inflammatory drugs or acetaminophen, as these were permissible analgesics in the study. The authors have, unfortunately, misinterpreted the data when they assert that there must have been many more cases of headache syndromes from our center that are missing from our case series. We entirely agree that the future role of rTMS in the treatment of migraine or other headache syndrome must await the results of large scale clinical trials, which are currently underway. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
