TY  - JOUR
DB  - APA PsycInfo <2008>
ID  - 2008-99180-211
T1  - The systemic nature of integrative healthcare: An exploration of clients' experiences in treatment.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Grimes, Erin M

Y1  - 2008//
N2  - Integrative treatment is defined as the use of a combination of conventional medical care with complementary or alternative treatment methods. Integrative treatment takes a systemic approach to treating symptoms by acknowledging the association between physiological and psychological problems. Current research has found many patients report physiological symptoms for which no known etiology or biological evidence can be discovered (Sobel, 1995). Clinicians are noting that symptoms persist despite the use of expensive medical procedures and antibiotics (Selhub, 2002). Factors such as stress, social support, self-esteem, psychological well-being, and quality of life have been associated with physical health, higher vulnerability to disease, and rate of healing. Recently, mind-body and alternative treatments have been utilized more frequently in the treatment, prevention, and recovery processes of complex health problems, including stroke, cancer, migraines, depression, and HIV. While treatment outcomes look promising, mechanisms illustrating how and why integrative treatment can impact symptoms and how physiological and psychological symptoms are related remains limited. Furthermore, knowledge about the uses of integrative treatment remain largely unknown among consumers and health care providers. This study attempted to learn about how integrative treatments are experienced by patients as a means to gain further understanding about specific factors that influence their sense of physical and emotional health and well-being. Through semi-structured qualitative interviews with patients who were involved in treatment at an integrative treatment center in San Diego, patients' experiences of treatment, their health status and its impact on their sense of identity as well as its impact on their methods of coping were explored. Challenges faced in the treatment process were also explored in the interviews. Additional quantitative data, assessing participants' quality of life, was gathered from the Abbreviated Version of the World Health Organization Quality of Life Scale (WHOQOL-BREF, The WHOQOL Group, 1995). The results of this study indicate that participants want information and resources to help them cope with their illnesses. Results also revealed clinicians' attitudes about healing influenced participants' feelings about their illness and treatment. Many participants reported that pursuing integrative treatment offered them resources to become pro-active in the treatment process, contributed to a sense of empowerment, and lead to feeling valued and understood. Results from the WHOQOL-BREF demonstrated that participants who endorsed a stronger sense of self, feeling empowered, and pro-active in qualitative interviews scored higher on the WHOQOL-BREF than those who did not endorse these qualitative themes. Severity of physical symptoms was not associated with quality of life scores on the WHOQOL-BREF. Future research should focus on factors that influence a positive experience in treatment, including clinicians' attitudes and patients' desire for information, coping methods, and ways to play a role in managing their health. Future research exploring ways in which specific integrative practices influence treatment outcomes ought to be further assessed. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
