Y1  - 2008//
N2  - Comments on an article by S. N. Ghaemi (see record 2008-16894-011). The doubts that Dr. Ghaemi raises in his erudite review by carefully questioning the purported efficacy of antidepressants in regulatory studies and real-world practice, and whether their effects are acute or long term, recognize the disillusionment that both patients with depression and the clinicians treating them experience in day-to-day practice. Dr. Ghaemi suggests that antidepressants, analogous to antibiotics, may in fact do harm when prescribed for extended periods of time. Dr. Ghaemi argues that the concept of major depressive disorder as an illness that comprises single depressive episodes of widely ranging severity as well as patterns of highly recurrent depression of varying duration is simply too broad. He further suggests that because nosology precedes pharmacology, priority should be assigned to accurate diagnosis. Ultimately, in addition to resolving current difficulties concerning the diagnosis of depression, elucidating its pathophysiology, and making better sense of the available therapeutic evidence, we need to demand more of antidepressants and have greater expectations with respect to treatment response, functional recovery, and relapse prevention. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
