li, Salvemini, D., Misko, T. P., Masferrer, J. L., Seibert, K., Curne, M. G., Needleman, P., Salvemini, Santos, R. A., Campagnole-Santos, M. J., Andrade, S. P., Santos, Santos, R. A., Campagnole-Santos, M. J., Baracho, N. C., Fontes, M. A., Silva, L. C., Neves, L. A., Ohveira, D. R., Caligiorne, S. M., Rodrigues, A. R., Gropen, J. C., Santos, Schafe, G. E., Bauer, E. P., Rosis, S., Farb, C. R., Rodrigues, S. M., LeDoux, J. E., Schafe, Schuchmann, S., Albrecht, D., Heinemann, U., Bohlen, Halbach, O., Schuchmann, Slanina, K. A., Roberto, M., Schweitzer, P., Slanina, Stellar, E., Stellar, Takemiya, T., Suzuki, K., Sugiura, H., Yasuda, S., Yamagata, K., Kawakami, Y., Maru, E., Takemiya, Tsvetkov, E., Carlezon, W. A., Benes, F. M., Kandel, E. R., Bolshakov, V. Y., Tsvetkov, Vane, J. R., Bakhle, Y. S., Botting, R. M., Vane, Vaughan, C. W., Vaughan, Vijitruth, R., Liu, M., Choi, D. Y., Nguyen, X., Hunter, R. L., Bing, G., Vijitruth, Bohlen, v., Halbach, O., Albrecht, D., Bohlen, Bohlen, v., Halbach, O., Albrecht, D., Bohlen, Bohlen, v., Halbach, O., Albrecht, D., Bohlen, Warner, T. D., Mitchell, J. A., Warner, Wright, J. W., Reichert, J. R., Davis, C. J., Harding, J. W., Wright, Yamagata, K., Andreasson, K. L., Kaufmann, W. E., Barnes, C. A., Worley, P. F., Yamagata
Y1  - 2007//
N2  - It is known from studies outside the brain that upon binding to its receptor, angiotensin-(1-7) elicits the release of prostanoids and nitric oxide (NO). Cyclooxygenase (COX) is a key enzyme that converts arachidonic acid to prostaglandins. Since there are no data available so far on the role of COX-2 in the amygdala, in a first step we demonstrated that the selective COX-2 inhibitor NS-398 significantly reduced the probability of long-term potentiation (LTP) induction in the lateral nucleus of the amygdala. Similarly, in COX-2-/- mice, LTP induced by external capsule (EC) stimulation was impaired. Second, we evaluated the action of angiotensin-(1-7) in the amygdala. In wild-type mice, angiotensin-(1-7) increased LTP. This LTP-enhancing effect of Ang-(1-7) was not observed in COX-2+/- mice. However, in COX-2-/- mice, Ang-(1-7) caused an enhancement of LTP similar to that in wild-type mice. The NO synthetase inhibitor L-NAME blocked this angiotensin-(1-7)-induced increase in LTP in COX-2-/- mice. Low-frequency stimulation of external capsule fibers did not cause long-term depression (LTD) in drug-free and angiotensin-(1-7)-treated brain slices in wild-type mice. In contrast, in COX-2-/- mice, angiotensin-(1-7) caused stable LTD. Increasing NO concentration by the NO-donor SNAP also caused LTD in wild-type mice. Our study shows for the first time that LTP in the amygdala is dependent on COX-2 activity. Moreover, COX-2 is involved in the mediation of angiotensin-(1-7) effects on LTP. Finally, it is recognized that there is a molecular cross-talk between COX-2 and NO that may regulate synaptic plasticity. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
