A2  - Baumann, P., Hiemke, C., Ulrich, S., Eckennann, G., Gaertner, I., Gerlach, M., et al., Baumann, De Bleeker, J. L., Vervaet, V. L., De Sarro, A., De Bleeker, Joos, A. A., Joos, Lucas, K. H., ScMiesser, S. H., O'Neil, M. G., Lucas, Weis, S., Karagulle, D., Komhuber, J., Bayerlein, K., Weis
Y1  - 2007//
N2  - A 32-year-old man with a history of DSM-IV recurrent depression for 10 years was admitted for a major depressive episode without psychotic features. Before admission the last medication consisted of 100mg fluvoxamine for 10 days in a stable dose. Up to then no side effects had occurred. After admission the dose of fluvoxamine was increased to 200mg within one day. Within four weeks of treatment under this regimen, the patient became euthymic. During his last week in hospital the patient developed an axillary furuncle. Psychiatric and neurological adverse effects have been reported with use of several antibiotics. Therefore, one should be careful when clindamycin is administered as an antibiotic medication to patients who are contemporaneously under treatment with fluvoxamine. Further investigations are needed. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
