A2  - Altamura, A. C., Bassetti, R., Bocchio, L., Santini, A., Mundo, E., Altamura, Candelaria, P. V., Backer, V., Laing, I. A., Porsbjerg, C., Nepper-Christensen, S., de Klerk, N., et al., Candelaria, Costa, P. T., McCrae, R. L., Costa, Dierynck, I., Bernard, A., Roles, H., De Ley, M., Dierynck, Hermans, C., Bernard, A., Hermans, Hori, H., Ohmori, O., Shinkai, T., Kojima, H., Nakamura, J., Hori, Laing, I. A., Goldblatt, J., Eber, E., Hayden, C. M., Rye, P. J., Gibson, N. A., et al., Laing, Laing, I. A., Hermans, C., Bernard, A., Burton, P. R., Goldblatt, J., Le Souef, P. N., Laing
Y1  - 2006//
N2  - Clara cell secretory protein (CC16) is an anti-inflammatory protein expressed in the respiratory tract. Several studies have suggested the association between CC16 and mental disturbances, such as schizophrenia, depression, and post-traumatic stress disorder. In the present study, we investigated the association between the CC16 gene A38G polymorphism and personality traits in 214 healthy Japanese subjects. Personality traits were evaluated by using the Revised NEO Personality Inventory (NEO PI-R) and the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI). As a result, no significant association was observed between the genotypes and the scores of the NEO PI-R or the STAI. The present results suggest that CC16 may not have a major role in the development of personality traits. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
