N2  - As other selective serotonine reupatke inhibitors (SSRI), sertraline has well-known gastrointestinal side effects, such as nausea, diarrhea, and vomiting. SSRIs increase the risk of gastrointestinal adverse effects in first-time users, as compared with tricyclic antidepressants. Although less frequent, SSRI-associated bleeding has also been observed, generally with predisposal or precipitant conditions, such as nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug treatments. The authors report the case of an adolescent girl who presented a hemorrhagic gastritis while she was treated by sertraline. This 13-year-old adolescent girl was hospitalized after her prolonged refusal to go to school associated with depressive symptoms, the dimension of social phobia and the severe depressive mood led to a sertraline prescription. A few days later, she was worried by the presence of blood in her emesis and consulted her pediatrician, who confirmed the hematemesis. The pediatricians concluded that the symptoms were a sertraline-induced gastritis and discontinued the antidepressant, replaced it by a tranquilizer, and prescribed sucralfate. Children and adolescents are generally more sensitive to adverse reactions, and this present case is an additional illustration that highlights the attention and caution needed in handling psychotropic drug treatments in children and adolescents. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
