A2  - Berson, D. S., Chalker, D. K., Harper, J. C., Leyden, J. J., Shalita, A. R., Webster, G. F., Berson, Cayce, K. A., Careccia, R. E., Hancox, J., Feldman, S. R., McMichael, A. J., Cayce, Habif, T. P., Habif, Jick, S. S., Kremers, H. M., Vasilakis-Scaramozza, C., Jick, Taylor, S. C., Cook-Bolden, F., Rahman, Z., Strachan, D., Taylor
Y1  - 2004//
N2  - Presents a short case study and differential diagnosis. It is followed by a discussion of the disease or condition and how the school nurse should handle it. C.B., a 16 year old male, was suffering from rashes on his chin and back. When his rashes did not improve after the use of over the counter products, he stopped using these. It got worse with time. On presentation his vital signs were normal. The rashes consisted of multiple papules, pustules, nodules, and a few scattered cysts on his back. This child has acne. Acne is so common that it usually does not bring a child to the school health office. Left untreated, this disease can cause severe scarring, disfigurement, and depression. Acne in teenagers begins as an overproduction of sebum. Successful treatment of acne is dependent on good patient compliance and a plan of care that considers the teenager's skin type. New treatment guidelines recommend starting with a topical retinoid. Topical and systemic antibiotics are commonly used to treat acne. Isotretinoin is a systemic vitamin A derivative used to treat severe nodulocystic or inflammatory acne, but there are several severe side effects with this product. Key points for school nurses are included. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
