T1  - Ibuprofen-induced pseudodementia.
A1  - Ginsberg, David L

A2  - Bernstein, A. L., Werlin, A., Bernstein, Browning, C. H., Browning, Griffith, J. D., Smith, C. H., Smith, R. C., Griffith, Gross, H. A., Dunner, D. L., Lafleur, D., Meltzer, H. L., Muhlbauer, H. L., Fieve, R. R., Gross, Hoppmann, R. A., Peden, J. G., Ober, S. K., Hoppmann, Jiang, H. K., Chang, D. M., Jiang, Katz, N., Zemishlany, Z., Weizman, A., Katz, Lantz, M. S., Giambanco, V., Lantz, Sussman, N., Magid, S., Sussman, Tharumaratnam, D., Bashford, S., Kahn, S. A., Tharumaratnam, Tollefson, G. D., Garvey, M. J., Tollefson
Y1  - 2003//
N2  - Both traditional nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), such as ibuprofen and selective cyclooxygenase (COX)-2 inhibitors, such as celecoxib, have been associated with psychiatric side effects including psychosis, delirium, agitation, and depression. The article presents a case report of pseudodementia associated with an NSAID, ibuprofen. Two weeks before going on an international trip, a 76-year-old man with mild glucose intolerance and osteoarthritis began ibuprofen 600 mg TID to reduce knee pain which occurred when he walked. Subsequently he was able to resume his usual physical activities. However, 3 weeks later his family noticed that he was confused, became lost in familiar places, and showed short-term memory loss. Upon returning home, the patient stopped taking ibuprofen, with complete resolution of his symptoms within 1 week. The patient experienced a second episode when six months later, before leaving on another international trip, the patient began taking ibuprofen. He discontinued ibuprofen 3 weeks later and within 1 week his mental state returned to normal. At evaluation 2 months after the second episode, the patient was alert and oriented, with clear, appropriate speech and a Mini-Mental State Examination score of 29/30. He was back to normal. The temporal sequences of events described above is consistent with a probable association between use of ibuprofen and pseudodementia. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
