Yamagata, Ying, S. W., Futter, M., Rosenblum, K., Webber, M. J., Hunt, S. P., Bliss, T. V., Bramham, C. R., Ying, Youdim, K., Martin, A., Joseph, J., Youdim
Y1  - 2003//
N2  - Long-term potentiation (LTP) and long-term depression (LTD) are two forms of activity-dependent synaptic plasticity thought to be involved in learning and memory. The inhibition of cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2), an enzyme that converts arachidonic acid to prostaglandins, has been shown to directly attenuate LTP in the dentate gyrus of the hippocampus. Recently the p38 mitogen activated protein (MAP) kinase cascade, a pathway utilised by cells for COX-2 expression, was implicated in LTD induction in the hippocampal CA1 region. Here we demonstrate a direct role for COX-2 and p38 MAP kinase in LTD and confirm the inhibitory role of COX-2 in LTP in the rat dentate gyrus. COX-2 inhibitor NS-398 (1 microM) perfusion 60 min before tetanic stimulation caused attenuation of LTD. Prolonged exposure (2 hr) to NS-398 caused a significant reduction in LTP. The p38 MAPK inhibitor, SB220025 (250 nM) significantly attenuated LTD compared to controls at 60 min but had no significant effect on LTP. Neither NS-398 or SB220025 had significant effect on the isolated NMDA-mediated excitatory postsynaptic potentials. These data demonstrate a role for COX-2 and p38 MAPK in LTD in the dentate gyrus independent of N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor activation. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
