Y1  - 2002//
N2  - There is evidence that major depression (MD) is associated with an up-regulation of the inflammatory response system (IRS). One of the major factors in this IRS activation is the hyperproduction of proinflammatory cytokines (CKs). Recent studies examined whether there is a causative role of these inflammatory mediators in the etiology of MD. Studies with animal models and CK immune therapy in humans suggest that pro-inflammatory CKs induce depressive symptomatology, which can be effectively reversed by antidepressant (ADT) treatment. This suggests that ADTs suppress pro-inflammatory CK production and/or action, resulting in improvement of depressive symptoms. The influence of ADTs on CK production has been examined in culture systems and in animal models of depression. Results suggest that ADTs of several classes decrease the production of pro-inflammatory CKs such as interferon-gamma and tumor necrosis factor-alpha, and increase that of interleukin-10, an anti-inflammatory CK. Further, the effect of ADT treatment on CK secretion and on plasma levels of CKs in depressed patients has been studied. Despite differences in methodology, current data indicate a normalization of CK plasma levels and production after ADT treatment. Implications for further research are discussed. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
