A2  - Anisman, H., Hayley, S., Turrin, N., Merali, Z., Anisman, Charlton, B. G., Charlton, Hart, B. L., Hart, Kenis, G., Maes, M., Kenis, Kronfol, Z., Kronfol, Leonard, B., Song, C., Leonard, Maes, M., Maes, Maes, M., Maes, Maes, M., Maes, Maes, M., Meltzer, H. Y., Scharpe, S., Cooreman, W., Uyttenbroeck, W., Suy, E., Vandervorst, C., Calabrese, J., Raus, J., Cosyns, P., Maes, Maes, M., Smith, R., Scharpe, S., Maes, Pollak, Y., Yirmiya, R., Pollak, Steptoe, A., Willemsen, G., Owen, N., Flower, L., Mohamed-Ali, V., Steptoe, Wichers, M., Maes, M., Wichers
Y1  - 2002//
N2  - This Special Section presents papers which review the current status of the relationship between the inflammatory response system (IRS) and major endogenous and organic depression. Studies published over the last 11 years reviewed here begin to test the conditions which are needed to accept the hypothesis that an activation of the IRS is involved in the pathophysiology and etiology of endogenous and organic depression. This hypothesis suggests that some types of endogenous and organic depression may be related to IRS activation, such as an increased production of pro-inflammatory cytokines, such as interleukin-beta (IL-lbeta), IL-6, tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha) and interferon-gamma (IFN-gamma). These cytokines are stress-sensitive, may cause depression, they have specific effects on brain systems involved in the pathogenesis of major depression, such as the serotonergic system and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, and their production may be suppressed by antidepressants. Future research should examine whether anti-inflammatory drugs are effective in the treatment of depression and whether naturally occurring variants of the IRS genes confer susceptibility to the development of the depressive phenotype through altered function of the respective gene products. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
