ID  - 1998-95012-225
T1  - The lived experience of spouses of individuals waiting for lung transplantation.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Kurz, Jane Mccausland

Y1  - 1998//
N2  - The use of lung transplantation for end-stage pulmonary disease has grown over the last decade and survival rates have increased secondary to improved surgical techniques, antibiotics and immunosupressive agents. This trend is expected to continue. Although issues surrounding lung transplantation have been studied from the patient perspective, only one other person has examined this from the view of the support person. This study was designed to explore the experience of waiting for a lung transplant from the perspective of the spouse. The purpose was to examine the stressors and coping mechanism affecting the spouse during the organ waiting period. Thirteen participants were volunteers who responded to either a letter sent out of a transplant center on the east coast or an informational page on a WEB site. This study used semi-structured telephone interviews and a mailed survey. Quantitative measures were the Family Inventory of Life Events (FILE) and the Center for Epidemiologic Studies Depression Scale (CES-D). Correlations were conducted between the FILE or CES-D scores and the total number of statements from the transcripts addressing events and depression. Data analysis followed the procedures of Grounded Theory. There was Little evidence of depression in the transcripts or the FILE scores. Four main categories resulted from clustering codes: negative stressors, positive factors, resources and coping. Further analysis supported the use of the Double ABCX Model of Stress and Adaptation as a guide for discussion of the categories. Limitations of the investigation were recognized. Recommendations for future research, practice, and policy were suggested. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2024 APA, all rights reserved)
