aes, Malouf, A. T., Malouf, Manelli, A. M., Puttfarcken, P. S., Manelli, Mattson, M. P., Barger, S. W., Furnkawa, K., Bruce, A. J., Wyss-Coray, T., Mark, C. J., Muck, L., Mattson, McGeer, P. L., Schulzer, M., McGeer, E. G., McGeer, Mehlhorn, G., Hollborn, M., Schliebs, R., Mehlhorn, Michelson, D., Stratakis, S., Hill, L., Reynolds, J., Galliven, B. S., Chrousos, G. P., Michelson, Mrak, R. E., Sheng, J. G., Griffin, W. S., Mrak, Nguyen, K. T., Deak, T., Owens, S. M., Kohno, T., Fleshner, M., Watkins, L. R., Maier, S. F., Nguyen, Quan, N., Whiteside, M., Herkenham, M., Quan, Raju, N., Raju, Ramamoorthy, S., Ramamoorthy, J. D., Pradad, P., Blat, G. K., Mahesh, V. B., Ramamoorthy, Rush, D. K., Aschimies, S., Merriman, M. C., Rush, Sehic, E., Szekely, A. L., Ungar, A., Oladehin, M., Blatteis, C. M., Sehic, Shen, Y., Connor, T. J., Nolan, Y., Kelly, J. P., Leonard, B. E., Shen, Shoji, Y., Vedono, Y., Ishikura, H., Takeyama, N., Tanaka, T., Shoji, Slansky, I., Herholz, K., Pietrzyk, U., Kessler, J., Grond, M., Mielke, R., Hein, H., Slansky, Sluzewska, A., Rybaakowski, J. W., Kaciak, M., Mackiewicz, A., Sobieska, M., Wiktorowicz, K., Sluzewska, Sluzewska, A., Rybakowski, J., Bosmans, E., Sobieska, M., Berghmans, R., Maes, M., Sluzewska, Solomon, G. P., Solomon, Song, C., Leonard, B. E., Song, Song, C., Lin, A., Banaccorse, S., Heide, C., Verkerk, R., Maes, M., Song, Suzuki, E., Shintani, F., Kamba, S., Asai, M., Nakaki, T., Suzuki, Xia, Z., de Poere, J. W., Nassberger, L., Xia, Yirmiya, R., Yirmiya
Y1  - 2001//
N2  - Reviews experimental and clinical evidence implicating the hypersecretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines in the onset of depression and Alzheimer's disease (AD). Activation and inhibition of the immune system occur in major depression. Many of the behavioral changes seen in depression are simulated by the pro-inflammatory cytokines interleukin-1 (IL-1), IL6, and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha), which may produce their effects by activating cyclooxygenase, nitric acid synthase, and corticotropin releasing factor. Effective antidepressant treatments largely attenuate immune system changes. Experimental and clinical evidence suggests that inflammatory processes in the brain caused in particular by TNF-alpha, together with the subsequent rise in free radicals, are instrumental in causing the pathological changes that underlie AD. The finding that nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs slow down the progression of the disease supports the inflammatory hypothesis. It is suggested that more research is needed into the interrelationships between the various pro-inflammatory cytokines and the behavioral changes invoked in major depression and AD. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
