A2  - Bork, P. M., Bacher, S., Schmitz, M. L., Kaspers, U., Heinrich, M., Bork, Chatterjee, S. S., Bhattacharya, S. K., Wonnemann, M., Singer, A., Muller, W. E., Chatterjee, Feuerstein, T. J., Gleichauf, O., Landwehrmeyer, G. B., Feuerstein, Fiebich, B. L., Heinrich, M., Langosch, J. M., Kammerer, N., Lieb, K., Fiebich, Fiebich, B. L., Hofer, T. J., Lieb, K., Huell, M., Butcher, R. D., Schumann, G., Schulze-Osthoff, K., Bauer, J., Fiebich, Frommberger, U. H., Bauer, J., Haselbauer, P., Fraulin, A., Riemann, D., Berger, M., Frommberger, Heuillet, E., Menager, J., Fardin, V., Flamand, O., Bock, M., Garret, C., Crespo, A., Fallourd, A. M., Doble, A., Heuillet, Kramer, M. S., Cutler, N., Feighner, J., Shrivastava, R., Carman, J., Sramek, J. J., Reines, S. A., Liu, G., Snavely, D., Wyatt-Knowles, E., Hale, J. J., Mills, S. G., MacCoss, M., Swain, C. J., Harrison, T., Hill, R. G., Hefti, F., Scolnick, E. M., Cascieri, M. A., Chicchi, G. G., Sadowski, S., Williams, A. R., Hewson, L., Smith, D., Rupniak, N. M., Kramer, Lieb, K., Fiebich, B. L., Berger, M., Bauer, J., Schulze-Osthoff, K., Lieb, Lieb, K., Schaller, H., Bauer, J., Berger, M., Schulze-Osthoff, K., Fiebich, B. L., Lieb, Linde, K., Ramirez, G., Mulrow, C. D., Pauls, A., Weidenhammer, W., Melchart, D., Linde, Maubach, K. A., Rupniak, N. M., Kramer, M. S., Hill, R. G., Maubach, Philipp, M., Kohnen, R., Hiller, K. O., Philipp
Y1  - 2001//
N1  - Biocenter Symposium on Drug Therapy.. Jun, 2000. U of Frankfurt. Germany. Presented at the aforementioned symposium.
N2  - Tested the hypothesis that extracts from St. John's wort interfere with protein synthesis induced by substance P (SP), a neuropeptide which has been implicated in the etiopathology of depression and anxiety. Using human astrocytoma cells, which express functional neurokinin-1-receptors, the authors investigated whether extracts from St. John's wort are able to inhibit SP-induced synthesis of the cytokine interleukin-6 (IL-6). It was found a potent and dose-dependent inhibition of SP-induced IL-6 synthesis by various extracts from St. John's wort. These results do not only give further evidence of the anti-inflammatory effects of St. John's wort, but also lend support to the hypothesis that the antidepressant effect of St. John's wort is, at least in part, a result of its inhibitory effects on SP-induced protein synthesis. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
