r, J. W., Vogel, W., Aulitzky, W. E., Wiedermann, C. J., Vannier, E., Huber, C., Dinarello, C. A., Tilg, Tilg, H., Shapiro, L., Vannier, E., Poutsiaka, D. D., Trehu, E., Atkins, M. B., Dinarello, C. A., Mier, J. W., Tilg, Valentine, A. D., Meyers, C. A., Kling, M. A., Richelson, E., Hauser, P., Valentine, Waguespack, P. J., Banks, W. A., Kastin, A. J., Waguespack, Whittington, R., Faulds, D., Whittington, World Health Organization, Yirmiya, R., Yirmiya
Y1  - 2001//
N2  - Studied the relationship between immune activation and the development of early depressive symptoms in 33 cancer patients (mean age 60 yrs) undergoing cytokine therapy. Ss were treated with subcutaneous IL-2 administered alone (n = 13) or in association with IFN-alpha (n = 5), or with IFN-alpha alone administered subcutaneously at low doses (n = 5) or intravenously at high doses (n = 10). The intensity of depressive symptoms was assessed during a clinical interview carried out before the start of cytokine therapy and 5 days later. On the same days, blood samples were collected for each S to measure serum concentrations of cytokines and cytokine-receptors. Results show that Ss treated with IL-2 or IL-2+IFN-alpha displayed concomitant mood symptoms and increased serum cytokine levels during treatment. In these Ss, the intensity of depressive symptoms at endpoint was positively correlated with the increases measured in serum levels of IL-10 between baseline and endpoint. The authors note that IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that is produced in response to the production of proinflammatory cytokines, and thereby reflects an inflammatory response. Results suggest a close relationship between depressive symptoms and the activation of the cytokine network. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
