T1  - Chronic pain in the elderly: Occupational adaptation as a means of coping with osteoarthritis of the hip and/or knee.
A1  - Klinger, Lisa
A1  - Spaulding, Sandi J
A1  - Polatajko, Helene J
A1  - MacKinnon, Joyce R
A1  - Miller, Linda

Y1  - 1999//
N2  - Determined the degree to which elderly patients with chronic pain from osteoarthritis of the hip or knee adapt their activities to help cope with the pain. 30 Ss (aged 65-88 yrs) were interviewed in the community, using the Social Support Scale (R. F. DeVellis et al, 1986), the Geriatric Depression Scale-Short Form (J. A. Yesavage et al, 1983), the Western Ontario and McMaster Universities Osteoarthritis Index (N. Bellamy, 1989), the Satisfaction with Life Scale (E. Diener et al, 1985), and the importance of Activities Rating Scale (N. R. Smith et al, 1986). Ss were also asked about their use of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). 87% reported they took medication, of which 37% took NSAIDs. All Ss reported making an adaptation to at least 1 activity, the mean number of adapted activities being 24, or about half of activities. Adaptations included changing ways the activity was performed, ceasing the activity, and lowering the frequency of the activity. Ss who lived with others reported greater life satisfaction and social support than those who lived alone. There was significant correlation between pain, adaptation, depression and social support. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
