T1  - Behavioral and psychological symptoms and signs in Alzheimer's disease.
A1  - Koponen, Hannu J
A1  - Saarela, Tuula
A1  - Erkinjuntti, Timo

Y1  - 1998//
N2  - Reviews the common psychiatric manifestations of Alzheimer's disease (AD) and their different treatment options. Behavioral and psychiatric symptoms discussed include agitation, anxiety, and depression. It is estimated that 5%-20% of AD patients suffer from major depression, 25%-35% from minor depression, and some 50% have depressive symptoms at some point. Psychotic symptoms have been demonstrated to occur in about 25% of patients with mild dementia and in about 50% of those with more severe AD. In some studies psychosis is associated with increased neurodegenerative changes in the cortex. Adequate treatment of AD begins with assessment of behavior, cognition, and functional status, and commonly used tools are briefly discussed. Pharmacological treatment may involve neuroleptics, benzodiazepines, antidepressants, and other agents. Current experimental approaches to slowing the rate of cognitive decline are discussed, including the use of antioxidants, MAO-B inhibitors, cholinesterase inhibitors, and anti-inflammatory agents. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
