T1  - Neuropathy associated with tricyclic antidepressants.
A1  - Benazzi, Franco
A1  - Ciucci, Gabriele

Y1  - 1997//
N2  - Presents the case of a 55-yr-old woman with chronic major depressive disorder and sporadic alcohol abuse who developed a painful neuropathy associated with tricyclic antidepressant treatment. When paresthesias appeared the S was taking desipramine, nortriptyline, alprazolam, and levothyroxine. Citalopram was added and the desipramine dose was reduced. However, during the following 2 wks bilateral, distal pain appeared, which rapidly became very severe. The S also noted some difficulty with limb movements. Pain and weakness gradually improved after psychopharmacological treatment was discontinued, and the S was treated with carbamazepine and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain relief. The authors suggest that it is worth remembering that tricyclics can rarely cause a severe neuropathy. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
