T1  - Tramadol hydrochloride: Something new in oral analgesic therapy.
A1  - Williams, H. James

Y1  - 1997//
N2  - Reviews properties of the analgesic tramadol hydrochloride (available in the US since 1995), including its pharmacologic and pharmacokinetic profile, efficacy for chronic and acute pain, dosing requirements, safety profile, and practical considerations for clinical use. Tramadol is a centrally acting, synthetic analgesic with weak opioid agonist properties and effects on neurotransmitter reuptake that explain its clinical actions. Studies show that tramadol is as effective as many oral analgesics currently in use, but without the tolerance or abuse potential of pure opioids and the gastrointestinal effects of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Further, excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and urinary retention are rare complications. Although its efficacy in severe pain is less, tramadol is indicated before morphine because of its safety. Its use in chronic conditions is not as well studied, but the toxicity profile remains its strength, and it has been recommended for long-term use in cancer patients by the World Health Organization. Tramadol is not recommended for use in children, and its use with the elderly needs more study. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
