T1  - Effect of present pain and mood on the memory of past postoperative pain in women treated surgically for breast cancer.
A1  - Tasmuth, Tiina
A1  - Estlanderb, Ann-Mari
A1  - Kalso, Eija

Y1  - 1996//
N2  - Examined whether the remembered intensity of postoperative pain (RIPP) after breast surgery was influenced by the development of chronic pain and if the RIPP had any correlation with the development of depression or anxiety. 93 breast cancer patients' estimated the severity of the RIPP 3 times in the year after surgery; the state anxiety and depression and the presence of pain in the ipsilateral arm were assessed before the operation, and 1, 6 and 12 mo after surgery. 53 Ss were treated with modified radical mastectomy with axillary clearance, and 40 with breast resection with axillary clearance. Ss were analysed in 3 groups according to the presence or absence of preoperative or chronic post-treatment pain. There was a significant correlation between the RIPP and the consumption of both opioids and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs on the ward. Ss who had chronic pain remembered having had more severe and increasing postoperative pain compared with Ss who had no chronic pain and decreasing RIPP. One yr after surgery anxiety and depression had returned to normal levels except in the patients who had chronic pain whose depression remained at a higher level during the first yr after surgery. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
