T1  - Lymphedema complicated by pain and psychological distress: A case with complex treatment needs.
A1  - Newman, Margo L
A1  - Brennan, Michael
A1  - Passik, Steven

Y1  - 1996//
N2  - One-third of lymphedema patients who experience pain report more functional interference, psychiatric symptoms, and distress related to intrusive and avoidant thoughts than patients without pain they pain also report higher levels of body image disturbance and decreased sexual drive. The case of a 55 yr-old divorced Hispanic woman who developed upper extremity lymphedema (UEL) following treatment for localized breast cancer was used to illustrate how pain can undermine rehabilitative efforts and detract from quality of life. S had a history of chronic musculoskeletal pain and recurrent major depression her pain ranged from 5-8 on a 0-10 scale, nonsterodial anti-inflammatory drugs prescribed did not ease pain. S became depressed and noncompliant in rehabilitation. S was put on Methadone, which relieved S's pain, lessened her depression and promoted adherence to rehabilitation. The most common causes of pain in patients with UEL are discussed. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
