T1  - Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and severe psychiatric side effects.
A1  - Browning, Charles H

Y1  - 1996//
N2  - Four male psychiatric outpatients (aged 42-67 yrs), 3 with affective disorders and 1 with schizophrenia, were treated with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDS) for a complaint of pain. The NSAIDS were withdrawn, then restarted for 3 patients, and then withdrawn again 1 or more times. The patients were evaluated while on and off NSAIDS. All 4 patients developed moderate to severe depression and 1 became severely paranoid while on NSAIDS initially. When NSAIDS were withdrawn there was remission of the depressive symptoms and in 1 case, the accompanying paranoia. The depressive symptoms were reproduced when NSAIDS were restarted in 5 instances. One patient also became paranoid in 2 instances. The paranoia remitted when the NSAIDS were discontinued. Findings suggest that NSAIDS can induce or exacerbate reproducible symptoms (depression, paranoia) in patients with either affective disorder or schizophrenia. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
