T1  - Risk factors for motor vehicle crashes among older drivers in a rural community.
A1  - Foley, Daniel J
A1  - Wallace, Robert B
A1  - Eberhard, John

Y1  - 1995//
N2  - Examined the health status of 206 drivers, aged 65+ yrs, involved in car crashes between 1985-1989. Ss completed instruments on physical functioning and prescription and over-the-counter medications, the Short Portable Mental Status Questionnaire, a recall memory test, and the Center for Epidemiologic Studies Depression Scale. Gender was more important than age in terms of annual crash risk. Ss with activities of daily living limitations, other function limitations, and visual or hearing deficits did not have a significant increase in crash risk, while those in the highest quintile for depressive symptoms and in the lowest tertile of words recalled did have a significant increase. Multivariate analyses showed back pain, use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory agents, but not antidepressants or other psychotropic medications, and poor recall memory to predict crash risk. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
