T1  - Critical review of data supporting affective disorder caused by nonpsychotropic medication.
A1  - Long, Teresa D
A1  - Kathol, Roger G

Y1  - 1993//
N2  - Reviews controlled and uncontrolled studies, comprehensive reviews with comparisons of controlled studies, and case reports where sheer numbers indicate a relationship between affective syndromes and nonpsychotropic medications. Findings suggest a relationship between the use of reserpine or lipophilic beta-blockers and depressive symptoms, but provide no clear evidence to support such a relationship with alpha-methyldopa, clonidine, calcium-channel blockers, or ACE-inhibitors. Glucocorticoids are related to both depressive symptoms and mania. Patients on anabolic steroids should be monitored for evidence of mania, rage, depression, and suicidality. There is no clear evidence that oral contraceptives are related to depressive symptoms. The review does not support a close relationship between affective disorders and use of H2 blockers, anticonvulsants, levodopa or antiarrhythmics, or antibiotics. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
