T1  - Factors influencing hospital patients' preferences in the utilization of life-sustaining treatments.
A1  - Cohen-Mansfield, Jiska
A1  - Droge, Janet A
A1  - Billig, Nathan

Y1  - 1992//
N2  - 97 elderly hospitalized patients (aged 64+ yrs) were asked about their preferences for several treatments (e.g., antibiotics, dialysis, permanent respirator) under 3 hypothetical levels of future cognitive functioning: intact, confused, and unconscious. Levels of cognitive functioning and depression were also assessed. 66% of the Ss were more likely to want treatment if they expected to be cognitively intact than when a future condition involved impaired cognition; 36% did not want any treatment in at least 75% of the conditions; and 16% wanted treatment in at least 75% of the conditions studied. 12% did not show any pattern in their preferences. The absence of a definite pattern was related to lower levels of education and to higher levels of depressive symptoms. Ss' self-reported preferences for treatments were influenced most by their personal values, religion, and experiences with illnesses of others. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
