T1  - The problems faced by general practitioners in the diagnosis and treatment of depression.
T2  - Problemes poses aux generalistes par le diagnostic et le traitement des depressions.
A1  - Lemperiere, T

Y1  - 1984//
N2  - Discusses reports that (a) most depressed individuals are treated by general practitioners and (b) these individuals constitute an important percentage of the generalist's clientele (10%, according to Western European data). It is argued that the general practitioner cannot easily accommodate the schizophrenic or chronic neurotic within the accustomed flow of his/her practice. The depressed patient, however, can be managed with prescribed drug therapy not unlike other familiar regimes (e.g., antibiotics, antihypertensive drugs). What general practitioners need is information concerning treatment strategies; this information should be presented in a form that is understandable and practical to apply. Simplified techniques for appropriate diagnosis are summarized, including the differential diagnosis of the different types of depression. Improved approaches for the communication of information about the effectiveness of antidepressant pharmacology are suggested. (15 ref) (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
