T1  - HYPOXOHYPERCAPNIC MELANCHOLIAS.
A1  - Bourgeois, Marc

Y1  - 1967//
N2  - SOCIETE MEDICO-PSYCHOLOGIQUE (FEBRUARY 27, 1967) PROCEEDINGS INCLUDE THESE OBSERVATIONS ON 2 PATIENTS WHOSE CONDITION WAS SO DOMINATED BY UNUSUAL PSYCHOTIC SYMPTOMS OF MELANCHOLIA THAT THE DIAGNOSIS OF ACUTE RESPIRATORY INSUFFICIENCY WAS ALMOST OVERLOOKED. THE ACUTE MELANCHOLIC STATE OF 1 S WAS IMMEDIATELY CURED BY REVIVING RESPIRATION AND NORMALIZING PULMONARY CONDITION AND BLOOD CHEMICAL MEASUREMENTS. THE USUAL TREATMENT OF OXYGEN THERAPY, AEROSES, AND ANTIBIOTICS WAS EMPLOYED. THE 2ND S RECOVERED FROM A PULMONARY INFECTION AFTER TREATMENT WITH ANTIBIOTICS BUT HIS MELANCHOLIC DEPRESSION WAS ONLY PARTIALLY REDUCED BY A THYMOANALEPTIC. HOWEVER, DURING A 2ND PULMONARY ATTACK, WHEN DRAMATIC SYMPTOMS OF ACUTE RESPIRATORY INSUFFICIENCY APPEARED, IMMEDIATE AND FINAL RECOVERY WAS OBTAINED ON NORMALIZATION OF PULMONARY CONDITION AND REDUCTION OF HYPOXOHYPERCAPNIC ACIDOSIS. SIGNS WHICH IDENTIFY THE ENCEPHALOPATHY AND REMARKS ON THE UNCERTAIN NATURE OF THE PHYSIOPATHOLOGY PRECEDE A DISCUSSION ON THE DIFFERENCES BETWEEN SYMPTOMATIC AND ENDOGENIC MELANCHOLIA. IT IS CONCLUDED THAT OF ALL THE MENTAL ILLNESSES, MELANCHOLIA, IN ITS MOST DEVELOPED FORM, IS THE ONE WHICH MOST OFTEN GIVES THE IMPRESSION OF BEING A PSYCHOSIS CAUSED BY FUNCTIONAL ENCEPHALOPATHY. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
